## Preamble

```sql
-- ============================================================================
-- PREAMBLE
-- ============================================================================

-- gen_random_uuid() is core in PostgreSQL 13+; the extension is created
-- defensively for compatibility with older engines and is a no-op on 16+.
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgcrypto;

CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS iam;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS organization;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS documents;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS workflow;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS tracking;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS records;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS notifications;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS audit;

-- Reserved Phase 2/3 namespaces — see "Reserved Phase 2/3 Schemas" below.
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS search_meta;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS portal;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS reporting;
```

---

## Shared Infrastructure

To support in-process asynchronous message reliability (ADR-B2-1), failed event handlers are logged to a shared dead-letter table. This table lives in the `public` schema.

```sql
-- Shared infrastructure table for event bus dead letters (ADR-B2-1).
-- Created in the public schema to avoid coupling with any specific domain schema.
CREATE TABLE public.event_bus_dead_letters (
    id              UUID NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    city_id         UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001'::uuid,
    event_id        UUID NOT NULL,
    event_type      TEXT NOT NULL,
    payload         JSONB NOT NULL,
    failed_module   TEXT NOT NULL,
    error_message   TEXT NOT NULL,
    failed_at       TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    retry_count     INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    deleted_at      TIMESTAMPTZ NULL,
    deleted_by      UUID NULL
);

-- Index on failed_at for pgboss queue scanning and cleanup
CREATE INDEX idx_event_bus_dead_letters_failed_at ON public.event_bus_dead_letters (failed_at);
```

